---
// Lesson 8.4 — Boss Attacks: Patterns & Phases
// Attacks are NEW prefabs the student builds, reusing the projectile pattern
// the student wrote themselves in Unit 3. Nothing in this project is a
// shockwave; Turret_Projectile.prefab has no collider; no projectile speed
// exists anywhere in the starter. Phase numbers live in a table.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>8.4 — Boss Attacks: Patterns &amp; Phases</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    The boss can be hurt (<a href="8.3.html">8.3</a>) and does not care. Today it starts
    answering. You are going to build the two attacks your spec promised, give each one a
    telegraph the player can read, and move every timing number into a phase table so that Phase
    3 is a change of <em>values</em> rather than a rewrite. The boss has no attacks in this
    project yet, and no projectile of its own — everything here is assembled from the pieces you
    have already built once.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">The Problem: An Unreadable Attack Is Not an Attack</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        A boss that damages you is trivial to build: put a number on a timer and subtract health.
        That boss is also unplayable, because the player's only possible response is to take the
        hit and hope. Every good attack is a <em>sentence with three parts</em>, and all three
        are timing, not geometry:
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Beat</th><th>What the boss is doing</th><th>What the player is doing</th><th>What the designer fixes</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Telegraph</strong></td>
            <td>Announcing the attack — a wind-up pose, a colour flash, a sound, a rearing back.</td>
            <td>Reading it, and starting the correct response.</td>
            <td>How long the tell lasts. Too short and the fight is unfair; too long and it is boring. This is the single most important number in the fight.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Strike</strong></td>
            <td>Committing — the wave travels, the body charges.</td>
            <td>Executing the answer: jumping, sidestepping, or already being somewhere else.</td>
            <td>What the answer <em>is</em>, and whether the answer is physically possible. Your spec's counterplay sentence lives or dies here.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Recovery</strong></td>
            <td>Unable to act — the boss is committed and vulnerable.</td>
            <td>Attacking. This is where the player's damage comes from.</td>
            <td>How long the boss is helpless. This number, more than any other, sets the pace of the whole fight.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <p>
        Notice what that table implies: <strong>the player's damage window is the boss's recovery
        time.</strong> If you never give the boss a recovery, you have built a fight where the
        only correct move is to run, and the only reason you win is patience. Read your
        <a href="8.1.html">8.1</a> spec again with that in mind — every attack you wrote has a
        recovery, whether you wrote one down or not.
    </p>

    <div class="callout info">
        <strong>What you already own.</strong> You built a turret in Unit 3 that detects the
        player by distance and fires projectiles on a fire-rate cooldown, and a projectile that
        spawns, travels, damages what it touches and destroys itself. That <em>is</em> the boss's
        ranged attack, wearing a different costume. You built an enemy in Unit 4 with a
        patrol / chase / attack state machine. That <em>is</em> the boss's charge. Nothing in
        this lesson is a new idea — it is your own code, re-pointed at a new target.
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Why Timing Is the Whole Design</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Write your spec's first attack as three numbers: telegraph seconds, strike seconds, recovery seconds. What is the player's damage window, expressed as one of those three?</li>
            <li>If the telegraph is shorter than a human's reaction time, what is the attack actually testing — skill, or luck? Look up a typical human reaction time and compare it to your number.</li>
            <li>If the recovery is longer than the telegraph, what does the fight become? Name a game you have played that does this deliberately.</li>
            <li>Your player has a jump you built in Unit 6 and a walk speed you chose in <a href="1.5.html">1.5</a>. Which of your two attacks is answered by jumping, and which by moving? What does each answer require the player to have <em>measured</em>?</li>
            <li>Phase 2 makes the boss faster. Which of the three beats does "faster" actually shorten — and which one must you never shorten, no matter how hard the fight gets?</li>
            <li>Your boss's attacks must be dodgeable in the arena you sketched in <a href="8.1.html">8.1</a>. Before you code anything, how many tiles of clearance does the player need for each attack?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Read Function-Level Examples</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        A telegraph is a promise made in advance. These five come from worlds where being early
        is the difference between a warning and an incident.
    </p>

    <p><strong>Example 1 — A level crossing (the barrier drops long before the train arrives):</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// The barrier comes down eight seconds before the train. The eight
// seconds is not slack - it is the whole safety system.</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 2 — A stage manager's cue call (a warning that is also an instruction):</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// "Standby LX 12... LX 12 GO." The standby is the telegraph;
// the GO is the strike. The operator is never surprised.</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 3 — Artillery ranging (one slow shot before the real one):</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// The first round is deliberately slow and obvious so the crew can
// adjust. It is not wasted - it is the telegraph.</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 4 — A press interlock (the cycle cannot be interrupted, only entered):</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// Once the press cycle starts it completes. The operator's only
// decision was made before the cycle began.</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 5 — A two-minute warning (the same event, announced earlier each time):</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// Same warning, same meaning, but the window to react is smaller.
// That is how a sport escalates without changing its rules.</code></pre>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Predict Before Building</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Example 5 escalates by shrinking the window without changing the rules. Which of your three beats will Phase 3 shorten, and what is the floor below which it must not go?</li>
            <li>Example 4 says the decision is made before the cycle begins. In your boss's charge, at what exact moment is the player's decision made — and can they change their mind after it?</li>
            <li>Example 1's eight seconds is a fixed, knowable number. Does the player need to <em>know</em> the number, or only to see the barrier move? What does that tell you about whether your telegraph should be a timer or a visual?</li>
            <li>Example 2 separates the standby from the GO. Which of your two attacks needs both, and which is fine with only the GO?</li>
            <li>If your boss attacks on a fixed interval with no telegraph at all, which example above is it accidentally copying — and why is that the wrong one for a boss fight?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Read the Docs: Three Ways to Schedule an Attack</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        "The boss attacks every three seconds" can be built at least three ways. All three are
        legitimate, and they are not equally expensive to change later. Pick one and defend it.
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Approach</th><th>What it looks like</th><th>What it costs</th><th>When it wins</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>(a) A timer in <code>Update</code></strong></td>
            <td>A float holding the last attack time, compared against <code>Time.time</code>. Exactly the shape you used for the turret's fire rate in <a href="3.3.html">3.3</a>.</td>
            <td>Every attack's state now lives in one method, and the boss will happily fire while it is mid-charge, mid-death, or after the player is gone, unless you guard each case.</td>
            <td>You have one attack and want it working in ten minutes.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>(b) A coroutine</strong></td>
            <td>A method that waits, telegraphs, strikes, recovers, and loops — reading top to bottom like the attack itself.</td>
            <td>You must keep a handle on it to stop it, or the boss keeps attacking after it dies. And an interrupted coroutine leaves the boss in whatever pose it was in.</td>
            <td>Your attack is a strict sequence of timed beats and you want the code to read like the timeline.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>(c) A state machine</strong></td>
            <td>The pattern you already built in <a href="4.5.html">4.5</a>: an enum, one state per frame, and transitions on conditions. <code>Telegraph</code>, <code>Strike</code>, <code>Recover</code> become three states.</td>
            <td>The most structure up front, and the most places to get a transition wrong.</td>
            <td>You want "the boss is vulnerable during recovery" to be a <em>property of the machine</em> rather than a flag you remember to set — and you already know how to write one.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <p>
        And separately: <strong>where do the numbers live?</strong> A phase change is not a
        behaviour change, it is a change of values. There are three ways to hold those values,
        and the cheapest one to tune is rarely the one people pick first:
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Approach</th><th>What it looks like</th><th>What it costs</th><th>When it wins</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>(a) Reassign fields on phase entry</strong></td>
            <td>The phase entry method assigns new values to attack interval, movement speed and so on, depending on which phase it is.</td>
            <td>The numbers are buried in code, so tuning Phase 3 means editing a script rather than a value. Two phases with the same value must both be written out.</td>
            <td>You have two phases, three numbers, and a deadline.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>(b) A table indexed by phase</strong></td>
            <td>One array or list of small records — one row per phase, one column per number — and a single reader that looks up the current phase's row.</td>
            <td>You must design the row's shape before you know all the columns you need, and changing that shape later touches every row.</td>
            <td>You can see the whole difficulty curve in one place, side by side, and tune it by comparing rows.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>(c) One asset per phase</strong></td>
            <td>A Unity <code>ScriptableObject</code> asset for each phase, holding that phase's numbers and referenced by the boss.</td>
            <td>A whole new kind of Unity asset to learn, and three files to keep straight on disk.</td>
            <td>You want a designer to tune the fight in the Inspector without opening the code at all.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Choose, Then Justify</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Which scheduling approach did you choose, and which of the three costs did you accept?</li>
            <li>If you chose a coroutine, what stops it when the boss dies? What is the visible symptom in the scene if you forget?</li>
            <li>If you chose a state machine, name the three or four states and write the transition condition on each arrow. Which arrow is the one the player is watching for?</li>
            <li>Which phase-value approach did you choose? Read your own code and answer honestly: to change Phase 3's attack interval, how many lines do you touch, and where are they?</li>
            <li>The phase table is the reason Phase 3 feels different without a tutorial. What does the player actually <em>perceive</em> when a number changes — and does your telegraph change too, or only the timing?</li>
            <li>Your spec said the fight is a certain length. With your recovery times fixed, what attack interval makes that arithmetic come out? Show the numbers.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab: Two Attacks and a Phase Table</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~30 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        <strong>Attack 1 — the travelling attack.</strong> Most specs have one. Build it the way
        you built your turret's projectile in <a href="3.2.html">3.2</a>: spawn it, let it travel,
        damage what it touches, destroy it on contact or after a lifetime. You already solved
        every one of those problems once. Two things are different, and both are yours to fix:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Nothing in this project is this attack yet.</strong> You are creating a new
            prefab. Do not assume a starting point exists — the <code>Turret_Projectile</code>
            prefab in <code>Assets/Prefabs</code> carries no collider, so even reusing it means
            adding one yourself, and its script is an empty file. Your own working projectile
            from Unit 3 is the better starting point.</li>
        <li><strong>The target is the player, not the boss.</strong> Your turret's projectile
            already damages the player through the signed-amount damage method your
            <code>StatManager</code> owns — the number you moved there in
            <a href="5.3.html">5.3</a>. Confirm which tag it checks before you re-point
            anything.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The whole thing reduces to three beats and one method that sets its heading:</p>
    <pre><code>public void Initialize(Vector2 direction)   // spawner sets the heading; the
                                            // projectile does not guess
// then: travel, damage on contact, destroy - all three you have written before</code></pre>

    <p>
        <strong>Choose the speed from your own numbers.</strong> There is no projectile speed to
        copy in this project — read the value you chose for your turret's projectile in
        <a href="3.3.html">3.3</a>. Your boss's attack should be <em>slower</em>, because it is a
        rhythm the player has to step out of, not a bullet they have to already be clear of. Then
        check the height against your jump: the player's apex, from <a href="6.3.html">6.3</a>,
        has to clear the attack's collider with room to spare. If it does not, one of those two
        numbers is wrong.
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>Attack 2 — the body attack.</strong> The boss's own collider is the weapon: it
        announces itself, commits to a direction, moves fast for a short time, then is helpless
        while it recovers. The direction is the same comparison your enemy's chase already makes
        in <a href="4.3.html">4.3</a>, flattened to one axis because the arena is flat. The
        recovery is the state where the player finally gets to use <a href="8.3.html">8.3</a>.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification: the attack the player must be able to answer.</strong>
        <ul>
            <li>What exactly does the player see during the telegraph? Name it as a thing on screen, not a variable.</li>
            <li>How long is the telegraph, in seconds, and how does that compare to a human reaction time? What is the player expected to do in that window — start moving, or already be moving?</li>
            <li>What is the counterplay sentence, with numbers in it: "the player ___s; it works because the attack is ___ and their ___ is ___."</li>
            <li>What does a mistake cost, in the player's own health — and what fraction of their maximum health is that? If the answer is "most of it", the fight allows almost no mistakes and your spec's fight length is a fantasy.</li>
            <li>During the recovery, the boss is vulnerable. How long is that window, and how many swings does <a href="8.3.html">8.3</a>'s cooldown fit inside it? If the answer is less than one, the player can never land a hit and the fight cannot be won.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification: the phase table.</strong>
        <ul>
            <li>List every number that changes per phase: attack interval, travel speed, telegraph length, recovery length, movement speed. Which of those must <em>not</em> change, no matter what?</li>
            <li>Where is that table stored, and how does the boss read the row for its current phase?</li>
            <li>Which numbers change at Phase 2, and which at Phase 3? If a number changes at both, why does it need two steps rather than one?</li>
            <li>Phase 3 adds a <em>new attack</em> as well as faster numbers. What in your table says "this attack is available from phase 3" — and what happens if you forget that guard?</li>
            <li>The phase entry from <a href="8.2.html">8.2</a> is guarded so it fires once. Does your table read happen there, or every frame? What is the cost of the wrong choice?</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="callout trap">
        <strong>The boss will keep attacking after it dies unless something stops it.</strong>
        This is the classic coroutine and timer bug: the death path destroys the object or stops
        the health logic, but the attack scheduler is running on its own and does not know. Watch
        for a wave that spawns from the boss's last position a second after the boss is gone. The
        fix is a guard on the scheduler, not a longer delay.
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Bug Hunt: The Unreadable Boss</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Pacing and Ownership</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Shrink the attack interval until it is shorter than your telegraph plus the player's reaction time. What is the fight now testing — and what is the smallest interval at which the fight is still fair?</li>
            <li>Spawn the attack at the boss's centre instead of at an offset in front of it. What does the attack touch on its first frame, and why does that matter when the thing it touches is tagged <code>Enemy</code> rather than <code>Player</code>? Now imagine the offset is wrong in the other direction and it spawns inside the player instead — what happens, and which fix from <a href="3.2.html">3.2</a> does that reuse?</li>
            <li>Let the body attack move with the same movement call your player uses. Read the documentation for your chosen movement method in <a href="2.2.html">2.2</a> and predict: does the boss stop at a wall, or pass through it? Test it, then argue whether a charge that ends against a wall is a bug or a gift to the player.</li>
            <li>Let Phase 3 trigger while an attack is already travelling. Does the one in flight change speed? Why does an already-spawned object keep the values it was born with — and when does that become a real problem rather than a curiosity?</li>
            <li>Kill the boss mid-attack. Does anything spawn afterwards? If a wave appears from an empty patch of floor, which of the three scheduling approaches in the table would have prevented it, and which one did you pick?</li>
            <li>Kill the <em>player</em> mid-telegraph. Does the boss still complete the swing — and is that the right behaviour for the game you are making? Argue it, then make the code agree with your argument.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Attack Rhythm</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Play your own boss. Can you beat it, how long did the fight take, and how many times were you hit? A boss the designer cannot beat is not hard, it is broken.</li>
            <li>Compare your measured fight length to the arithmetic you wrote in <a href="8.1.html">8.1</a>. Which number was wrong — the boss's health, your damage, or your recovery times? Change exactly one and re-time it.</li>
            <li>Write a third attack as a <em>spec sentence only</em> and do not code it: "the arena hazard wakes on a timer during phase 3". Why does a written attack cost nothing while a coded one costs a session?</li>
            <li>Give the telegraph a second, non-visual channel — a sound, a camera nudge — and play the fight with your eyes half-closed. Did you dodge more or fewer attacks?</li>
            <li>Commit and push with a message naming the lesson: <code>"8.4: two telegraphed attacks + phase table"</code>.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Name the three beats of an attack and say which one is the player's damage window</li>
            <li>Defend your choice of scheduling approach, and say what one of the other two would have cost you</li>
            <li>Explain how the body attack reuses your own enemy chase logic, and how the travelling attack reuses your own projectile</li>
            <li>Say the counterplay sentence for each attack with real numbers in it, including your own jump height and walk speed</li>
            <li>Explain what the phase table changes and what it must never change</li>
            <li>Beat your own boss, and quote the fight time</li>
            <li>Commit and push</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-08/index.html">Unit 8: Boss Fight</a> — attack patterns and telegraphs</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-04/enemy-state-machines.html">Enemy State Machines</a> — the machine your boss extends</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — coroutines, <code>Time</code> and instantiation</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="8.5.html">8.5 — Win, Lose &amp; the Death Loop</a>, where the fight has to end.</p>
</div>`;
